 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Last season Fforme designer Frances Howie was looking to the bygone era of rarefied socialites—yielding a collection of rich textures and shapely, almost swaddling silhouettes. This season she went in the opposite direction.</p><p>“It’s about this fast-paced 1920s, 1930s moment—or the ’60s even,” she said at a preview of the collection. “It’s geometric, sharper. She’s got even less structure; she’s very linear. It’s a shorter length because we’re going places, we’re on the street.”</p><p>Shown in an airy, sun-strewn loft in West Chelsea, there were great looks reduced to their taut essence—slim, paneled pants with ribbed tank tops, say, or straight-line tunic dresses with deep-V fronts shown over trousers. But that was only part of the story. “I wanted to look at this idea of futurism and modernity,” the designer said, plus continue her ongoing exploration of American sportswear.</p><p>It’s hard to say exactly how some of these inspirations expressed themselves, but the truth is, it doesn’t matter much—what matters are the clothes, and they were quite good. The opening papery coat with wide sleeves that stood slightly away from the body and a slightly rounded shoulder and a creased button-up shirt had a wonderful sculptural quality. Dark, slim coats made from panels of lining fabric and leather that fastened at the waist were simple and smart, as were airy trenches—in cobalt blue, then white—that flowed behind the bare-faced models like capes. Transparency was a theme, as with a silver, sleeveless dress of netting—or others made from lacy squiggles—shown over sporty maillots, while some transparent knit columns came with the intarsia outline of a low-cut dress woven in (and left open at the side for a little erotic edge). Fringe, too, was a motif, seen at the neckline and hem of a dress, or along a jacket’s edge, or dancing along the side of a pair of loose trousers.</p><p>Materials were sourced from Italian mills and included wools traditionally used for men’s suiting and cotton-silk blends that were light but kept their shape. One slip dress was made of lamé and looked like golden liquid poured onto the body. There was a surprising amount of body in the collection—shirts with deep vents in the back or dresses with cutaways at the side and fastened with a touch of leather strap. Necklaces crafted of coins or small bottles on a chain were inspired by the British stylist Judy Blame. Shoes were flat slippers or sandals.</p><p>Despite being relatively stripped back, there was just the right sense of shape and fine-tuned detail in the collection. “We don’t really think of ourselves as minimalist,” Howie said. “We think of ourselves as considered. So there’s an attention to detail and good, real clothing that’s been made in a masterful way and with integrity.”</p></div>",
